<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext, and what actually restricts it.

  The app needs plain HTTP for exactly one thing: handing a sealed envelope to
  the user's own PC at 192.168.x.x or similar, when both are on the same Wi-Fi.

  ⚠ The obvious config for that cannot be written. Android's <domain> matches a
  hostname or one exact address - it does NOT accept CIDR, so
  "10.0.0.0/8" here would match nothing at all and the local path would simply
  never work, silently. That was tried first and checked against the docs
  before it shipped.

  So the honest position, rather than a comment claiming otherwise:

    * cleartext is permitted by default here, because Android gives no way to
      permit it for private ranges only, and
    * what actually restricts it is Lan.java, which resolves each candidate
      address and refuses anything that is not on one of this phone's own
      networks before it opens a connection. There are exactly two http:// uses
      in this app and both are behind that check.

  What this file CAN do, and does: pin the one address that leaves the building
  to encrypted only. The relay can never be downgraded to plain HTTP by a bug,
  a redirect, or a future edit - it will fail instead.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">xniperbuilds.com</domain>
    </domain-config>

</network-security-config>
